# Shell scripts keep LF line endings in the working tree on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so a checkout on
# a Windows machine rewrites LF to CRLF on the way to disk. The committed blobs
# stay clean either way -- this is a working-tree conversion, and it happens
# again on every checkout, so fixing a file by hand does not hold.
#
# That is harmless for almost everything here. Python does not care (universal
# newlines, and nothing is run via its shebang), route files are parsed with
# splitlines() and strip(), and Markdown is Markdown.
#
# It is not harmless for a shell script, because deploy.sh rsyncs the working
# tree to the car and rsync copies bytes. A CRLF script lands on Linux with a
# shebang the kernel reads literally: it looks for an interpreter named `bash\r`
# and reports `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`. car_health.sh is the one
# that matters -- it is documented to run ON the car, and it is what you reach
# for after the car drops off the network, which is the worst moment to find it
# broken.
#
# Scoped to *.sh deliberately. A blanket `* text=auto` would renormalize the
# whole repo; this changes no blob at all, since they are already LF.
*.sh text eol=lf
